Opposite sides of a parallelogram give equal vectors, so $\overrightarrow{AD}=\overrightarrow{BC}$ in parallelogram $ABCD$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.2,2.1);
\coordinate (D) at (1.2,2.1);
\draw[->,thick] (A) -- (B);
\draw[->,thick] (D) -- (C);
\draw[->,thick] (A) -- (D);
\draw[->,thick] (B) -- (C);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
$ABCD$ is a parallelogram with $\overrightarrow{AB}=\underset{\sim}{u}$ and $\overrightarrow{AD}=\underset{\sim}{v}$. Write $\overrightarrow{AC}$ in terms of $\underset{\sim}{u}$ and $\underset{\sim}{v}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.2,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.4,2.2);
\coordinate (D) at (1.2,2.2);
\draw[->,thick] (A) -- (B) node[midway,below] {$\underset{\sim}{u}$};
\draw[->,thick] (A) -- (D) node[midway,left] {$\underset{\sim}{v}$};
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (D) -- (C);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In the diagram $\overrightarrow{OA}=\underset{\sim}{a}$, $\overrightarrow{OB}=\underset{\sim}{b}$ and $M$ is the midpoint of $AB$. Write $\overrightarrow{AM}$ in terms of $\underset{\sim}{a}$ and $\underset{\sim}{b}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
